# Part 1 - SSH Brute Force Detection

## Step 1 - First Attempt: A Rule That Looked Right but Wasn't

The initial rule chained off Wazuh's default SSH authentication-failure rule (`5760`) with no frequency threshold.

```xml
<rule id="100020" level="12">
  <if_sid>5760</if_sid>
  <description>Custom: SSH authentication failed</description>
  <mitre><id>T1110.001</id></mitre>
  <group>authentication_failed,ssh,bruteforce,attack</group>
</rule>
Problem Identified

This rule fired on every single failed login, including one-off password typos, rather than detecting an actual burst pattern.

Tagging a single failure as brute force (T1110.001) was inaccurate and would have flooded the dashboard with false positives.

Step 2 - Corrected Rule With Frequency/Timeframe Correlation

The rule was changed to require multiple failures from the same source within a defined time period.

<rule id="100020" level="12" frequency="6" timeframe="120">
  <if_matched_sid>5760</if_matched_sid>
  <same_source_ip />
  <description>Custom: SSH brute force - 6 failed logins in 2 min</description>
  <mitre><id>T1110.001</id></mitre>
  <group>authentication_failed,ssh,bruteforce,attack,</group>
</rule>

frequency="6" and timeframe="120" require six matches from the same source IP within 120 seconds before this rule fires.

This is what allows the rule to detect a brute-force pattern rather than isolated authentication failures.

Step 3 - Test

The rule was tested by manually generating six or more failed SSH login attempts in under two minutes from a test source.

The Wazuh Dashboard confirmed that:

The underlying authentication-failure rule (5760) detected the individual failures.
The custom rule (100020) fired after the required frequency threshold was reached.
The custom alert represented the correlated brute-force activity rather than a single failed login.
Part 2 - Reflected XSS Detection
Step 1 - Rule Definition

A custom rule was created for reflected XSS activity against DVWA.

<rule id="100012" level="8">
  <if_sid>31105</if_sid>
  <description>meduilm-HIGH: XSS attempt detected against monitored application</description>
  <group>attack,web,xss_attempt,</group>
</rule>

The rule chains off Wazuh's stock web-attack rule group (31105), which already includes generic XSS pattern matching against the Apache access log.

Step 2 - Test

The test was performed against DVWA's Reflected XSS module at Low security.

The following payload was submitted:

<script>alert('SentinelForge-Test')</script>

The test confirmed:

The JavaScript alert fired in the browser, confirming that the reflected XSS payload executed.
Rule 100012 fired in Wazuh Discover.
The payload was visible in the expanded alert's URL field.
Step 3 - Payload Variation Testing

Additional payload variations were tested:

<img src=x onerror=alert('XSS2')>

and:

These variations were used to confirm that the underlying decoder/rule was not only matching the literal <script> string, but could identify a broader XSS-pattern group.

Troubleshooting
Problem - Brute-Force Rule Fired on Every Single Failure
Cause

The initial rule had no frequency, timeframe, or same_source_ip correlation.

Solution

Frequency and source-IP correlation were added to the rule:

frequency="6"
timeframe="120"

along with:

<if_matched_sid>5760</if_matched_sid>
<same_source_ip />

This changed the detection from identifying individual authentication failures to identifying a repeated brute-force pattern.

Verification Checklist
 SSH brute-force rule fires only on the correlated burst, not single failures.
 Brute-force rule is correctly tagged with T1110.001.
 XSS rule fires on a DVWA reflected XSS payload.
 XSS detection is confirmed against multiple payload variants.
 Both rules are visible in Wazuh Discover with the correct rule ID and description.
Lessons Learned
1. A Rule That "Fires" Is Not Automatically a Correct Detection

The initial SSH rule successfully generated alerts, but it detected individual authentication failures rather than a brute-force pattern.

Matching the pattern — burst behavior — rather than the symptom — a single failed login — is what separates a real detection from unnecessary noise.

2. MITRE Technique Tags Should Reflect the Actual Detection

MITRE ATT&CK technique tags should reflect what the rule actually detects, not simply where the rule is chained from.

Mislabeling a single authentication failure as T1110.001 would have been misleading in an audit of the ruleset.

The corrected rule detects repeated credential failures from the same source, making T1110.001 appropriate for the intended brute-force detection.

3. Test Against Payload Variations

Testing against payload variants, rather than only one canonical payload, builds confidence that the rule generalizes instead of simply pattern-matching one specific string.

Multiple XSS payload structures were tested to verify the underlying detection was not dependent on a single <script> payload.

